some ets => (one ets => lone ets.admins else none) else none)\n- Step 4: Ensure that you do not use a let-binding to bind a set and then reference an element variable inside the set definition (which is not allowed in Alloy 6).\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix eliminates the scoping error by ensuring all variable references are valid and using Alloy 6's conditional and quantification constructs correctly; it is distinct from prior attempts that only bracketed or rephrased the let-binding without fixing the underlying variable scoping issue.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen using let-bindings in Alloy, do not attempt to introduce a variable inside the bound set and reference it outside its legal scope; instead, use explicit quantification or conditional expressions to access elements of the set.",
