    "evaluator_feedback": "[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe error \"The name 'timeoutT' cannot be found\" at line 232 arises because the let-bound variable timeoutT is not in scope inside the implication consequent at lines 231–234. In Alloy, let-bound variables are only in scope within the block in which they are defined. In the R1R2 predicate, the let timeoutT binding is nested within an all et: EmergencyTrigger | {...} block, but the implication at line 231–234 is not properly enclosed within the same scope; thus, timeoutT is not visible at line 232. Previous fixes attempted to adjust let syntax, use curly braces, or rewrite quantification, but did not address the scoping issue. The real issue is that the let-bound variable timeoutT must be in scope for every expression that references it.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nMove the let timeoutT binding to the outermost block of the all et: EmergencyTrigger quantification so that timeoutT is in scope for all relevant expressions.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: In pred R1R2, locate the block:\n  ```\n  all et: EmergencyTrigger | {\n      let start = et.at |\n      let timeoutT = ... |\n      ...\n  }\n  ```\n- Step 2: Merge the let-bindings for start and timeoutT into a single let-block at the top of the all et: EmergencyTrigger block, using Alloy 6's multi-variable let binding:\n  ```\n  all et: EmergencyTrigger | let start = et.at, timeoutT = TO/next[TO/next[TO/next[TO/next[TO/next[start]]]]] | {\n      ...\n  }\n  ```\n- Step 3: Remove any nested let-bindings for start or timeoutT now made redundant.\n- Step 4: Ensure that all references to timeoutT within the block (including at line 232) are inside the scope of this outer let-block.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it uses a multi-variable let binding at the top level of the quantification block, ensuring that timeoutT is in scope wherever referenced; this approach is distinct from prior failed attempts which only adjusted let syntax or quantification structure without correcting the scoping.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen using let-bindings in Alloy, especially for variables needed in multiple expressions, bind them as early and as high up in the block as possible to ensure proper scope throughout.",
