    "evaluator_feedback": "[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe root cause of the type error is that the local variable session in the let binding shadows the State field session. In the quantified expression (session in s.session ...), Alloy interprets s.session as a field access, and session as the local variable, resulting in an invalid relational join (State . Session) because s.session is a Session and session is also a Session atom. This shadowing leads to a parsing ambiguity and type mismatch.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nRename the local variable session in the let binding within fact EmergencyModeDurationAndExit to avoid shadowing the State field session.\n\n[USER GUIDANCE COMPLIANCE]:\n- Guidance: \"local variable named session and session in s.session have the same name, leading to Alloy interprets s.session as a relational join between s as a state and session as a Session, therefore the error message. Rename the local variable so it does not shadow the field name.\" -> implemented by Step 1\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: In fact EmergencyModeDurationAndExit (lines 156–175), rename the local variable session in the let binding to a different name (e.g., triggerSession). Specifically, change:\n  \n  all et: EmergencyTrigger | let session = et.session, start = et.at | { ... }\n  \n  to:\n  \n  all et: EmergencyTrigger | let triggerSession = et.session, start = et.at | { ... }\n\n- Step 2: In the body of this fact, replace every occurrence of session (referring to the local variable) with triggerSession. For example, modify lines 170 and 173 to read:\n  \n  (triggerSession in s.session and TO/gte[s.t, et.endedAt]) => s.mode = NormalMode\n  ...\n  (triggerSession in s.session and TO/gte[s.t, start] and TO/lt[s.t, et.endedAt]) => s.mode = EmergencyMode\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it removes the name shadowing that was causing the parser to misinterpret the expression, ensuring that the correct variable is referenced, and follows the user's explicit guidance to resolve the type error.\n\n[LESSON]:\nAvoid using local variable names in let bindings that shadow or duplicate field names within the same scope in Alloy, as this can cause parsing ambiguities and type errors.",
