    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: keep fix\nRationale: User review has fully clarified the requirements and timing semantics; the previous diagnosis and requirement rewrites are confirmed. Model and predicates need to be realigned to the clarified requirements.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Unsatisfiable (All_Requirements, R1R2R3R4, DelegationCoverage UNSAT)\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertR4_EmergencyDelegationsRevoked, assertDelegationCoverage, assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs)\n- Instances: N/A (no model instance satisfies all requirements)\n- Vacuity: None (assertions and predicates are meaningful; failure is substantive, not vacuous)\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: The model remains UNSAT due to a mismatch between the clarified requirements and the current predicate logic; model repair is now possible and necessary to align DelegationCoverage and revocation facts with the explicit user-provided semantics.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n- Update the DelegationCoverage predicate to separately witness: (a) at least one delegation made and active during Emergency, and (b) at least one delegation made and active during Normal mode. Do NOT require that a single delegation instance persist from Emergency into Normal.\n    Example split (Alloy pseudocode):\n    ```\n    pred DelegationCoverage {\n        some d1: Delegation | d1.active and d1.mode = Emergency\n        some d2: Delegation | d2.active and d2.mode = Normal\n    }\n    ```\n- Update model facts so that all delegations and emergency privileges granted during Emergency mode are revoked before the first time step of Normal mode (i.e., at the Emergency/Normal transition).\n- Remove or adjust any predicates or facts that require persistence of Emergency delegations into Normal mode.\n- Add or update assertion tests to confirm that no Emergency-mode delegation or privilege remains active in any Normal mode state.\n- Re-verify: Ensure all assertion runs now pass and that the new DelegationCoverage predicate is satisfiable.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n1. Relax the revocation constraint so that emergency delegations may persist for one additional state after Emergency ends; rerun DelegationCoverage to see if it becomes SAT.\n2. Separate coverage into two predicates: (a) at least one delegation exists during Emergency, (b) at least one during Normal (regardless of origin); run each alone to confirm which is blocked.\n3. Temporarily remove R4.2 and rerun All_Requirements to check if the model becomes SAT—if so, the contradiction is confirmed.\n4. Add a predicate that witnesses a delegation both created and revoked during Emergency, to test if revocation is being enforced at the correct transition.\n5. Run with increased Time/State bounds (e.g., 6-7) to rule out scope limitation, though evidence suggests this is not the cause.\n\n```\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: inconsistency\n- Issue: Previous contradiction between revocation timing (immediate at Emergency end) and DelegationCoverage (requiring cross-mode persistence).\n- Rationale: User has now clarified that cross-mode persistence is NOT required and revocation must be immediate.\n- Action: Update DelegationCoverage and all model predicates to eliminate the expectation of cross-mode delegation persistence.\n\n- Issue type: ambiguity (resolved)\n- Issue: Timing of revocation for emergency privileges and delegations at Emergency/Normal boundary.\n- Rationale: User has specified that all must be revoked before the first Normal mode time step, with no delay.\n- Action: Ensure model now enforces this strict revocation at the Emergency/Normal transition.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: Emergency delegations are revoked strictly at the Emergency/Normal transition (no overlap).\n  Category: keep\n  Rationale: User has explicitly confirmed this requirement.\n\n- Assumption: DelegationCoverage requires a delegation made during Emergency to persist into Normal mode (for coverage).\n  Category: reduce\n  Rationale: User has explicitly denied this; DelegationCoverage must witness separate delegations in each mode.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R4.2 (\"All roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit from Emergency mode...\")\n- Issue Type: ambiguity / inconsistency (now resolved)\n- Evidence: User review confirms immediate revocation required; previous model/predicate logic assumed possible persistence, causing contradiction.\n- Recommended Update: Explicitly state in R4.2: \"All roles and privileges delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked at the Emergency/Normal transition, so that none remain active in any Normal mode state.\"\n- Implication: Model must ensure all Emergency-mode delegations and privileges are revoked before Normal mode begins; any persistence across modes is forbidden.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: DelegationCoverage predicate\n- Issue Type: ambiguity / overconstraint (now resolved)\n- Evidence: User clarified that DelegationCoverage should witness separate delegations active in Emergency and Normal modes, not require a single delegation to persist across modes.\n- Recommended Update: Redefine DelegationCoverage to require: (a) at least one delegation active during Emergency mode, and (b) at least one delegation active during Normal mode, without requiring them to be the same instance.\n- Implication: The model can now satisfy both coverage and revocation requirements without contradiction.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ1-Q4: confirmed - User has provided explicit answers clarifying all previously ambiguous or blocking requirements.\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Update model predicates and facts to strictly enforce immediate revocation of all Emergency-mode delegations and privileges at the Emergency/Normal transition.\n- Redefine DelegationCoverage as per the clarified semantics: require delegation presence in each mode, not persistence across modes.\n\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): \n- Add assertion tests to confirm no Emergency-mode delegations or privileges remain active during any Normal mode state.\n- Ensure documentation for requirements and predicates is updated to reflect clarified revocation and coverage behavior.\n\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): \n- Consider explicit modeling of revocation events at the Emergency/Normal transition for auditability.\n- Explore test cases for edge scenarios (e.g., delegation granted and revoked at the last Emergency time step).\n```",
