    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: escalate to requirement clarification\nRationale: Persistent unsatisfiability and counterexamples remain due to ambiguities and contradictions in delegation coverage, emergency privilege revocation, and now additional constraints clarified by the user (e.g., self-delegation prohibition, direct-role delegation, mutex handling). Further model refinement without resolving these at the requirement level will not converge.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Unsatisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples found (assertDelegationCoverage, assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs)\n- Instances: Meaningful for baseline; N/A for failing predicates\n- Vacuity: None\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: Hard metrics fail due to persistent UNSAT predicates and assertion counterexamples. Additional user-specified requirements introduce new constraints not yet modeled or verified, deepening the need for requirement-level clarification and model adaptation.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n1. Incorporate newly clarified user requirements:\n   - Prohibit self-delegation: Add a constraint/fact ensuring no user can delegate a role to themselves.\n   - Restrict delegation to directly assigned roles: Add a constraint ensuring users can only delegate roles they are directly assigned (not via delegation).\n   - Prohibit delegation of delegated roles: Enforce in model that roles acquired via delegation cannot be further delegated.\n   - Record request processor: Ensure all delegation/revocation requests, whether approved or rejected, log the identity of the processor.\n   - Emergency mode entry: Enforce that Emergency mode activates iff exactly two administrators trigger it while in Normal mode.\n   - Delegation expiry: Add constraint that expiry time of any delegated role must be strictly after the delegatedAt time.\n   - Mutex roles: Ensure mutual exclusivity is symmetric, roles are paired, and a role cannot be mutually exclusive with itself.\n   - Emergency mode mutex bypass: Enforce that if a user holds two mutex roles during Emergency, at least one must be via delegation.\n\n2. Split DelegationCoverage into two predicates:\n   - One requiring at least one active Emergency-mode delegation in Emergency states.\n   - One requiring at least one active Normal-mode delegation in Normal states.\n\n3. Run DelegationCoverage variants independently to isolate which mode's coverage is unsatisfiable (Diagnostic Experiment #1).\n\n4. Remove DelegationCoverage from R1R2R3R4 and rerun to confirm consistency of remaining requirements (Diagnostic Experiment #2).\n\n5. Add/modify fact to test if Emergency-mode delegations can persist one tick into Normal, and rerun assertions (Diagnostic Experiment #3).\n\n6. Variant of assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs: Allow one-tick lag in revocation, rerun to see if assertion passes (Diagnostic Experiment #4).\n\n7. Strengthen EmergencyDelegationAndPrivilegeRevocation fact: Ensure s.emergencyAdmins = none in all NormalMode states.\n\n8. Update the model and verification suite after requirement clarifications.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n1. Run DelegationCoverage with only the Emergency or only the Normal mode requirement to see which side cannot be satisfied (isolate minimal unsat).\n2. Run R1R2R3R4 with DelegationCoverage removed to confirm if the rest of the requirements are consistent.\n3. Add a diagnostic predicate that allows Emergency-mode delegations to persist one tick into Normal mode to test effect of strict vs. lax revocation.\n4. Run a variant of assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs that permits a one-tick lag in revocation to see if assertion then passes.\n5. Separate DelegationCoverage into two predicates: one for Emergency, one for Normal, and run independently.\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: ambiguity\n- Issue: Delegation coverage—whether required at all times or as an existence property.\n- Rationale: Requirements do not specify if coverage must always hold or just be witnessed; current model and requirements are inconsistent.\n- Action: Clarify if coverage is a safety property (always required) or a witness property (only needs to occur at least once).\n\n- Issue type: ambiguity\n- Issue: Timing of emergency privilege revocation at Emergency/Normal transition.\n- Rationale: Not specified whether revocation must be atomic or if a short overlap is allowed.\n- Action: Specify if any transient overlap is permitted or if revocation must be immediate.\n\n- Issue type: inconsistency\n- Issue: DelegationCoverage vs. Emergency delegation revocation—if all Emergency-mode delegations are revoked instantly, Normal mode may lack coverage, causing unsatisfiability.\n- Rationale: Model cannot satisfy both unless cross-mode persistence or separate per-mode coverage is clarified.\n- Action: Specify if cross-mode persistence is required or if coverage is per mode only; clarify revocation semantics.\n\n- Issue type: requirement gap\n- Issue: Prohibition of self-delegation and delegation of delegated roles not previously specified in requirements.\n- Rationale: User explicitly indicated these constraints, but they are not yet formally required or modeled.\n- Action: Update requirements to prohibit self-delegation and delegation of delegated roles.\n\n- Issue type: requirement gap\n- Issue: Delegation limited to directly assigned roles not formally captured.\n- Rationale: User requirement; not enforced in current model or requirements.\n- Action: Explicitly require delegation is allowed only for directly assigned roles.\n\n- Issue type: requirement gap\n- Issue: Mutation/exclusivity symmetry and pairings for mutex roles not fully specified.\n- Rationale: User clarified mutual exclusivity must be symmetric and paired; not currently formalized.\n- Action: Update requirements to specify mutual exclusivity must be symmetric, paired, and roles cannot be mutex with themselves.\n\n- Issue type: requirement gap\n- Issue: Emergency mutex role bypass—at least one mutex role must be delegated if both are held in Emergency mode.\n- Rationale: Not specified in initial requirements, now clarified by user.\n- Action: Add explicit requirement and corresponding model constraint for Emergency mode mutex bypass.\n\n- Issue type: requirement gap\n- Issue: Logging of request processor for all (approved/rejected) requests not specified.\n- Rationale: User requires processor to be recorded; missing from requirements/model.\n- Action: Add explicit requirement for logging processor identity for all requests.\n\n- Issue type: requirement gap\n- Issue: Delegation expiry must be after delegation time.\n- Rationale: User specified; not yet formalized in requirements/model.\n- Action: Add explicit requirement and model constraint.\n\n- Issue type: requirement gap\n- Issue: Emergency mode entry requires exactly two triggering administrators while in Normal mode.\n- Rationale: User clarified; not yet in requirements/model.\n- Action: Update requirements and model.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\nNone - assumptions appear sound\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R4.2 (Emergency-mode delegation revocation)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity / inconsistency\n- Evidence: UNSAT Matrix, counterexamples, and user clarification.\n- Recommended Update: Specify if Emergency-mode delegations may persist into Normal and, if not, adjust DelegationCoverage to be per mode.\n- Implication: Resolves cross-mode unsatisfiability; model and predicates can be aligned accordingly.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R3.3 / R4.1 (Emergency privileges assignment/revocation)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity\n- Evidence: Counterexamples, UNSAT Matrix, and user clarification.\n- Recommended Update: Specify if revocation must be atomic or if a grace period is allowed.\n- Implication: Determines whether assertions should enforce strict or relaxed revocation timing.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: DelegationCoverage predicate\n- Issue Type: ambiguity / logical gap\n- Evidence: UNSAT Matrix, counterexamples, and current model behavior.\n- Recommended Update: Clarify if per-mode witness is sufficient or if cross-mode persistence is required; update model and predicates.\n- Implication: Drives assertion and model structure.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Self-delegation prohibition\n- Issue Type: missing requirement\n- Evidence: User explicit requirement.\n- Recommended Update: Add requirement that a user cannot delegate a role to themselves.\n- Implication: Model and constraints must enforce this rule.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Delegation of delegated roles prohibition\n- Issue Type: missing requirement\n- Evidence: User explicit requirement.\n- Recommended Update: Add requirement that only directly assigned roles may be delegated.\n- Implication: Model must exclude delegation chains.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Delegation restricted to directly assigned roles\n- Issue Type: missing requirement\n- Evidence: User explicit requirement.\n- Recommended Update: Add requirement limiting delegation authority to direct assignees.\n- Implication: Model must enforce direct role assignment as delegation prerequisite.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Request processor logging\n- Issue Type: missing requirement\n- Evidence: User explicit requirement.\n- Recommended Update: Add requirement to record request processor for all requests.\n- Implication: Model must ensure logging for all request outcomes.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Emergency mode entry\n- Issue Type: missing requirement\n- Evidence: User explicit requirement.\n- Recommended Update: Add requirement that Emergency mode is triggered only by exactly two administrators while in Normal mode.\n- Implication: Model must enforce this transition rule.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Delegation expiry constraint\n- Issue Type: missing requirement\n- Evidence: User explicit requirement.\n- Recommended Update: Add requirement that delegated role expiry is strictly after delegation time.\n- Implication: Model must enforce this temporal ordering.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Mutex role symmetry and pairing\n- Issue Type: missing requirement\n- Evidence: User explicit requirement.\n- Recommended Update: Specify mutual exclusivity must be symmetric, paired, and roles cannot be mutex with themselves.\n- Implication: Model and requirements must match these invariants.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Emergency mutex role bypass requirement\n- Issue Type: missing requirement\n- Evidence: User explicit requirement.\n- Recommended Update: Add requirement that in Emergency mode, if a user holds two mutex roles, at least one must be delegated.\n- Implication: Model and verification must enforce this constraint.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ43_3: confirmed - The distinction between witness coverage in Emergency vs. Normal mode is explicitly confirmed as requiring separate delegations per mode, not cross-mode persistence.\nQ43_4: confirmed - Strict, atomic revocation of emergency privileges at Emergency end is required, no delay allowed.\nQ43_2: confirmed - All delegations made during Emergency must be revoked precisely at the Emergency/Normal transition, no grace period.\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Clarify and update requirements to resolve ambiguities in delegation coverage and emergency privilege revocation timing.\n- Incorporate and formalize all newly specified user requirements (self-delegation prohibition, direct-role delegation only, prohibition of delegation of delegated roles, processor logging, Emergency mode entry criteria, delegation expiry, mutex role symmetry, Emergency mutex bypass).\n- Split DelegationCoverage predicates for Emergency and Normal modes and update verification approach to per-mode witnesses.\n\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): \n- Update model/constraints to enforce new and clarified requirements.\n- Strengthen EmergencyDelegationAndPrivilegeRevocation to require s.emergencyAdmins = none in all NormalMode states.\n\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): \n- Add diagnostic and variant predicates to flexibly test requirement boundaries (e.g., short revocation lags, mutex role assignment scenarios).\n- Document clarified requirements, model rationale, and verification results for maintainability.\n```",
