    "evaluator_feedback": "[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe error arises in the holdsRoleDirectly predicate due to the use of \"some directAssignment: User -> Role | ...\" within a predicate. In Alloy, local variable declarations (like \"some ... | ...\") inside a predicate introduce existential quantification, not a global or model-wide relation. The model comments indicate that directAssignment is intended to represent a (possibly global) direct user-to-role assignment relation, but it is not actually declared anywhere in the model. This results in the predicate attempting to quantify over all possible assignments, which is a form of higher-order quantification Alloy cannot skolemize or analyze, hence the error. Previous failed fixes did not address the lack of a declared directAssignment relation, which is the root cause.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nDeclare directAssignment as a global relation at the top level so that the predicate can reference it directly, avoiding higher-order quantification.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: At the top level of your model (preferably after the sig Role and sig User declarations), add the following line to declare directAssignment as a relation:\n    ```alloy\n    directAssignment: User -> Role\n    ```\n  This can be as a top-level fact or as a field of a new sig, or simply as a global relation (since Alloy 6 allows top-level relations).\n- Step 2: In the holdsRoleDirectly predicate, replace\n    ```\n    some directAssignment: User -> Role | \n        (u -> r) in directAssignment and ...\n    ```\n    with\n    ```\n    (u -> r) in directAssignment and ...\n    ```\n- Step 3: Ensure that any references to directAssignment throughout the model are now direct (not locally quantified), and that the relation is now globally available for Alloy to analyze.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix explicitly declares the directAssignment relation at the global scope, removing the need for existential quantification over relations (which Alloy cannot analyze), and aligns with Alloy 6's requirements for relational navigation and analysis.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen a relation is meant to represent a fundamental system structure, it must be declared at the global scope, not introduced via quantification inside predicates. Quantifying over relations (i.e., higher-order quantification) is not supported in Alloy's analysis.",
